145
00:07:14,176 --> 00:07:14,836
The terminal,

146
00:07:16,437 --> 00:07:18,837
or more specifically the shell.

147
00:07:19,818 --> 00:07:20,598
What is the shell?

148
00:07:20,958 --> 00:07:35,862
Well computers have a bunch of different interfaces you can use to interact with them right we're all familiar with graphical user interfaces or GUIs those are the ones where you move your mouse around and you click things and those are fine they work really well if they're well designed graphical user interfaces.

149
00:07:36,182 --> 00:07:40,143
We have other things more recently as well we have things like agentic interfaces,

150
00:07:40,263 --> 00:07:42,144
we have voice interfaces,

151
00:07:42,224 --> 00:07:42,724
we have

152
00:07:43,872 --> 00:07:45,313
like virtual reality,

153
00:07:45,493 --> 00:07:47,094
augmented reality type interfaces.

154
00:07:47,214 --> 00:07:49,595
Maybe we'll get neural interfaces at some point.

155
00:07:49,615 --> 00:07:51,376
We have all of these ways to interact with the computer.

156
00:07:51,836 --> 00:07:56,998
But the reality is that a lot of those interfaces are sort of specialized.

157
00:07:57,098 --> 00:08:03,001
They're specialized to what the author of that interface had in mind when they created the interface.

158
00:08:03,301 --> 00:08:03,822
So for a GUI,

159
00:08:03,862 --> 00:08:04,362
for example,

160
00:08:04,382 --> 00:08:08,564
the only things you can do are the things that are programmed into that program to do.

161
00:08:09,876 --> 00:08:10,376
Similarly,

162
00:08:10,476 --> 00:08:14,778
it's pretty hard to take two different programs that are doing maybe related tasks and you wish you can combine them.

163
00:08:14,998 --> 00:08:17,178
To try and actually combine them is often pretty hard.

164
00:08:17,678 --> 00:08:25,881
It requires there to be some custom implementation of software A from vendor A with software B from vendor B so that those two can interoperate.

165
00:08:27,161 --> 00:08:33,443
And sometimes you really just want to drop into one level lower where everything is just you can write whatever commands you want,

166
00:08:33,444 --> 00:08:34,883
you can chain them together however you want,

167
00:08:35,063 --> 00:08:36,143
and that is what the shell is.

168
00:08:36,163 --> 00:08:38,424
It is the textual interface to the computer.

169
00:08:38,924 --> 00:08:41,425
This is the thing that we sort of had from the very beginning.

170
00:08:42,145 --> 00:08:44,666
This is what preceded all the GUIs,

171
00:08:44,686 --> 00:08:46,126
it preceded all the other interfaces,

172
00:08:46,406 --> 00:08:50,347
and it is in a way the sort of core language for interacting with the computer.

173
00:08:51,968 --> 00:08:59,129
The shell is really a program on your computer that lets you give inputs commands and prints the outputs of those commands,

174
00:08:59,269 --> 00:09:00,530
and that's how you interface with it.

175
00:09:01,230 --> 00:09:03,251
The shell runs in the context of a terminal.

176
00:09:03,471 --> 00:09:06,892
A terminal you can think of as like the GUI window that is around it.

177
00:09:07,692 --> 00:09:08,472
So in this case

178
00:09:08,732 --> 00:09:10,813
you can see that there's sort of a border around this thing.

179
00:09:10,913 --> 00:09:11,753
That is my terminal.

180
00:09:11,993 --> 00:09:13,114
It doesn't have any UI.

181
00:09:13,634 --> 00:09:14,334
Some terminals do,

182
00:09:14,514 --> 00:09:15,095
mine does not.

183
00:09:15,555 --> 00:09:17,736
And inside of that terminal runs a shell.

184
00:09:18,696 --> 00:09:20,677
You can open a terminal on your computer.

185
00:09:21,457 --> 00:09:24,699
Most computers these days ship with some kind of terminal,

186
00:09:24,799 --> 00:09:25,919
with some kind of shell.

187
00:09:26,579 --> 00:09:26,960
On Linux,

188
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:29,300
you can usually open this with like Ctrl + Alt + T,

189
00:09:29,541 --> 00:09:30,761
tends to work on most distros,

190
00:09:31,041 --> 00:09:33,422
or you can open your application launcher and just search for terminal,

191
00:09:33,462 --> 00:09:34,523
you should probably find something.

192
00:09:35,263 --> 00:09:35,843
On Windows,

193
00:09:35,844 --> 00:09:37,724
you type the Windows key + R

194
00:09:37,988 --> 00:09:39,209
And then you can type either

195
00:09:39,929 --> 00:09:41,410
cmd or powershell.

196
00:09:41,750 --> 00:09:43,831
Those will open the same terminal,

197
00:09:43,832 --> 00:09:44,772
but with different shells.

198
00:09:45,272 --> 00:09:45,932
And on Mac OS,

199
00:09:46,473 --> 00:09:48,033
Command + Space to open Spotlight,

200
00:09:48,053 --> 00:09:48,954
and you search for Terminal,

201
00:09:49,094 --> 00:09:49,674
you get a terminal.

202
00:09:50,555 --> 00:09:51,055
Now,

203
00:09:51,615 --> 00:09:52,176
the terminal,

204
00:09:52,376 --> 00:09:54,177
you usually don't need to worry too much about.

205
00:09:55,037 --> 00:10:00,000
It gives you things like interactions with operating system things like copy-paste or mouse-paste

206
00:10:00,140 --> 00:10:01,061
movement and selection.

207
00:10:01,681 --> 00:10:02,361
But apart from that,

208
00:10:02,742 --> 00:10:05,523
most of the interesting stuff here is going to happen in the shell.

209
00:10:06,744 --> 00:10:11,867
The shell that is most commonly used across the board is the Bourne Again SHell or bash.

210
00:10:12,467 --> 00:10:15,909
Bash is what you're going to get on Linux usually when you start a terminal.

211
00:10:16,790 --> 00:10:18,391
On Mac OS it also used to be bash,

212
00:10:18,551 --> 00:10:21,012
these days it's something called Zsh or ZSH.

213
00:10:21,712 --> 00:10:24,174
This is also now the default on some

214
00:10:24,514 --> 00:10:25,555
Linux distributions as well.

215
00:10:25,995 --> 00:10:27,696
Zsh is a bash compatible shell.

216
00:10:28,016 --> 00:10:31,139
So things that are valid in Bash are usually valid in Zsh,

217
00:10:31,419 --> 00:10:36,463
and Zsh has a bunch of other ergonomic improvements and feature improvements that make it easier to interface with.

218
00:10:37,424 --> 00:10:40,927
You'll find that there are actually a lot of different shells that are often built for different purposes.

219
00:10:41,287 --> 00:10:41,787
And in fact,

220
00:10:42,328 --> 00:10:42,969
there are some that,

221
00:10:43,149 --> 00:10:49,174
like,
Bash is pretty old and has not seen significant new feature expansion since then.

222
00:10:50,215 --> 00:10:52,117
Zsh does have a lot of ergonomics improvements,

223
00:10:52,118 --> 00:10:55,680
but there are also shells like fish that are intended to be...

224
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:00,942
written by humans on the command line rather than by programs or in programs.

225
00:11:01,822 --> 00:11:03,383
And you'll find that if you talk to the instructors,

226
00:11:03,423 --> 00:11:06,064
like all of us use different shells for different reasons.

227
00:11:06,884 --> 00:11:10,446
And this is also something where you could choose to explore your own shell down the line.

228
00:11:10,706 --> 00:11:14,047
But for the initial sort of investigation and for everything we cover in this class,

229
00:11:14,247 --> 00:11:17,669
we're going to be focusing on bash and sort of by extension also Zsh.

230
00:11:19,650 --> 00:11:20,250
On Windows,

231
00:11:20,410 --> 00:11:22,451
you will not get a bash compatible prompt.

232
00:11:22,471 --> 00:11:23,691
You will get either batch,

233
00:11:24,231 --> 00:11:25,352
B-A-T-C-H

234
00:11:26,236 --> 00:11:27,417
or you will get PowerShell,

235
00:11:27,457 --> 00:11:29,057
the newer version of the shell for Windows.

236
00:11:29,597 --> 00:11:32,478
These have similar concepts,

237
00:11:32,578 --> 00:11:34,579
so what you find in Bash and similar shells,

238
00:11:35,119 --> 00:11:36,079
but they are kind of different.

239
00:11:36,099 --> 00:11:37,219
Their syntax is kind of different,

240
00:11:37,220 --> 00:11:39,000
the way you invoke programs is kind of different.

241
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:41,280
We will not cover those in this class.

242
00:11:41,861 --> 00:11:46,042
If you're on Windows I recommend you install either the Windows subsystem for Linux,

243
00:11:46,502 --> 00:11:47,002
WSL,

244
00:11:47,282 --> 00:11:51,443
or you install a Linux virtual machine that lets you get access to a proper shell.

245
00:11:51,863 --> 00:11:52,804
PowerShell is also very good,

246
00:11:52,824 --> 00:11:53,844
but for very different reasons.

247
00:11:54,604 --> 00:11:55,264
But in this class,

248
00:11:55,324 --> 00:11:57,926
we'll focus entirely on Bash and its derivatives.

249
00:11:59,246 --> 00:11:59,726
Now,

250
00:11:59,726 --> 00:12:01,167
why should you care about the shell?

251
00:12:01,927 --> 00:12:02,287
Well,

252
00:12:02,287 --> 00:12:04,568
it's usually much faster than clicking around.

253
00:12:04,908 --> 00:12:06,029
Once you get used to the shell,

254
00:12:06,189 --> 00:12:09,210
you can do quite a lot of things with very few keystrokes.

255
00:12:09,530 --> 00:12:10,171
Whereas in a GUI,

256
00:12:10,551 --> 00:12:12,212
it tends to become more laborious,

257
00:12:12,252 --> 00:12:14,052
especially the more complicated your task is.

258
00:12:14,432 --> 00:12:15,873
And part of this is because in the shell,

259
00:12:16,133 --> 00:12:16,974
you can automate.

260
00:12:17,334 --> 00:12:19,895
The shell is essentially a programming language that,

261
00:12:20,175 --> 00:12:21,055
you know,
the command line,

262
00:12:21,095 --> 00:12:22,596
the prompt that you see up here

263
00:12:22,836 --> 00:12:24,396
where I get to type things and they appear,

264
00:12:24,897 --> 00:12:31,999
that prompt is essentially the start of a file that I get to write.

265
00:12:32,079 --> 00:12:34,560
That is a bash program that gets executed.

266
00:12:34,980 --> 00:12:36,400
Most of those programs are very simple.

267
00:12:36,401 --> 00:12:38,081
They're just start a program of some kind.

268
00:12:38,321 --> 00:12:41,662
But you can write fairly complicated expressions here as we'll get into later.

269
00:12:42,842 --> 00:12:45,483
There are a couple of other reasons why the shell is useful.

270
00:12:45,743 --> 00:12:47,824
One of them is this ability to combine programs.

271
00:12:47,844 --> 00:12:48,644
We'll see that as well.

272
00:12:48,884 --> 00:12:52,486
how you can take the output of one program and feed it into another program that does something else,

273
00:12:52,487 --> 00:12:54,287
and then feed it into a third program that does something else.

274
00:12:54,487 --> 00:12:58,769
The shell is very much built for that kind of combinatorial thinking if you will.

275
00:13:00,370 --> 00:13:04,332
Knowing your way around the shell is also useful for interacting with the open source community.

276
00:13:04,713 --> 00:13:07,634
You'll find that for almost any open source tool you find,

277
00:13:07,934 --> 00:13:09,375
the instructions for building it,

278
00:13:09,635 --> 00:13:10,236
installing it,

279
00:13:10,356 --> 00:13:10,856
running it,

280
00:13:10,976 --> 00:13:13,438
are all going to be things that happen in your shell.

281
00:13:13,718 --> 00:13:15,239
You also increasingly find things like,

282
00:13:15,459 --> 00:13:16,620
"How do I do this on my computer?"

283
00:13:16,720 --> 00:13:19,342
and you'll be told to open a terminal and type something in there.

284
00:13:20,042 --> 00:13:26,107
And usually it's useful to know roughly what that command does rather than just blindly copy-pasting things from Stack Overflow and going,

285
00:13:26,108 --> 00:13:27,067
"Yeah,
it's probably fine."

286
00:13:27,588 --> 00:13:34,392
So hopefully this gives you a bit more sort of intuition and insight into what those things actually do and which things you should be worried about and which things are not.

287
00:13:35,213 --> 00:13:38,895
There's also increasingly use of shell in

288
00:13:39,776 --> 00:13:40,717
and Bash in particular,

289
00:13:40,937 --> 00:13:44,359
in configuring continuous integrations for projects.

290
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:50,063
You'll find this both in the open source community and also in the closed source community if you end up taking a job at a company.

291
00:13:50,524 --> 00:13:54,306
You'll find that a lot of the configuration for how the software is built behind the scenes,

292
00:13:55,007 --> 00:13:55,988
how it is tested,

293
00:13:56,068 --> 00:13:57,709
how all of those pipelines flow together,

294
00:13:58,029 --> 00:14:00,491
is also all done using shell commands.

295
00:14:01,832 --> 00:14:04,314
So let's then look into what is the shell?

296
00:14:04,654 --> 00:14:07,316
Well so as I already explained here we have the prompt.

297
00:14:07,696 --> 00:14:10,639
The prompt is the main interface you have to your shell.

298
00:14:11,739 --> 00:14:13,141
And it tells you,

299
00:14:13,321 --> 00:14:16,023
this sort of start of the shell prompt over here,

300
00:14:16,503 --> 00:14:19,686
tells you that my username is John.

301
00:14:20,066 --> 00:14:22,949
It tells you that the host name of the machine I'm on is XOS.

302
00:14:23,950 --> 00:14:26,292
The colon is the separator just like the ampersand.

303
00:14:26,592 --> 00:14:28,213
And the thing after the squiggly line,

304
00:14:28,214 --> 00:14:28,714
the tilde,

305
00:14:29,094 --> 00:14:31,556
is the location I am at in my file system.

306
00:14:31,936 --> 00:14:33,858
Tilde is short for my home directory.

307
00:14:34,138 --> 00:14:35,940
So currently I'm in my home directory.

308
00:14:36,848 --> 00:14:44,011
Is what this prompt is telling me the dollar is telling me that I'm not the root user I'm not the admin user in this shell usually you'll just see dollar here,

309
00:14:44,012 --> 00:14:52,974
and you don't have to worry too much about it And then it's just sort of friendly waiting for me to run a command now when you run a command Why is it why does it sound double when I'm standing over here?

310
00:14:53,414 --> 00:14:54,054
It's disturbing

311
00:14:57,215 --> 00:14:58,156
That's still on maybe

312
00:15:00,576 --> 00:15:01,076
No?

313
00:15:01,496 --> 00:15:01,996
Weird.

314
00:15:02,217 --> 00:15:03,377
Is there a secret microphone?

315
00:15:04,017 --> 00:15:04,678
Oh,
well.

316
00:15:04,818 --> 00:15:05,318
It'll be OK.

317
00:15:07,699 --> 00:15:12,361
So on the prompt you can type commands that you want to be executed.

318
00:15:12,362 --> 00:15:15,682
And the simplest kind of command you can type is just the name of a program.

319
00:15:15,962 --> 00:15:16,563
So for example,

320
00:15:17,183 --> 00:15:18,123
there's a program called date.

321
00:15:18,483 --> 00:15:22,725
And perhaps unsurprisingly when I run the date program,

322
00:15:23,065 --> 00:15:24,786
it prints the current date to my terminal.

323
00:15:25,186 --> 00:15:28,848
And then I'm dropped back in a prompt where I can now type more things.

324
00:15:29,968 --> 00:15:32,550
We can also execute commands with arguments.

325
00:15:32,750 --> 00:15:33,250
So,

326
00:15:33,250 --> 00:15:37,413
arguments here are things that follow the program name in the command.

327
00:15:37,773 --> 00:15:38,354
So for example,

328
00:15:38,355 --> 00:15:44,078
the echo program is one that will take the arguments we pass to it and print them back out.

329
00:15:44,578 --> 00:15:46,500
So for example if I type "hello world"

330
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:47,060
here,

331
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:48,241
it prints out "hello world".

332
00:15:48,741 --> 00:15:52,564
So hello and world here are arguments to the echo program.

333
00:15:54,245 --> 00:15:58,208
It's worth spending a few seconds here on what argument parsing is.

334
00:15:58,824 --> 00:16:02,906
Argument parsing in Bash is that you take the string,

335
00:16:03,006 --> 00:16:04,467
the text that the user gave you,

336
00:16:04,867 --> 00:16:07,188
and you split it at white space boundaries.

337
00:16:08,168 --> 00:16:12,490
And each white space separated word is one argument,

338
00:16:12,810 --> 00:16:15,992
and the first argument is the command of the program to execute.

339
00:16:16,812 --> 00:16:21,054
Now there are ways to say that I want an argument that has white space in it.

340
00:16:21,374 --> 00:16:24,015
So you can do quoting just like you can in most other programming languages.

341
00:16:25,852 --> 00:16:27,633
echo prints all of its arguments,

342
00:16:27,634 --> 00:16:29,153
so it's not going to be particularly useful there.

343
00:16:29,453 --> 00:16:30,814
But I could for example say

344
00:16:31,874 --> 00:16:32,474
"hello world"

345
00:16:32,774 --> 00:16:40,536
like this and it will print the same thing because echo defaults to printing space separated of its arguments which is the same thing I'm providing here.

346
00:16:40,976 --> 00:16:48,359
But for example if I put more spaces in here you'll see that now this gets treated as one argument so it gets printed as a single thing.

347
00:16:48,739 --> 00:16:53,340
If on the other hand I typed hello and then a bunch of spaces in world

348
00:16:53,728 --> 00:16:55,510
These are just two arguments to echo.

349
00:16:55,890 --> 00:16:57,311
They get separated as two arguments.

350
00:16:57,391 --> 00:16:58,572
The white space is ignored,

351
00:16:58,592 --> 00:16:59,453
it's just a separator.

352
00:17:00,113 --> 00:17:02,635
And then those two arguments are provided to echo and it prints them back out.

353
00:17:04,036 --> 00:17:11,743
Argument parsing is something you're going to run into a lot when you start writing command lines yourself because sometimes getting the quoting right can be quite annoying.

354
00:17:14,084 --> 00:17:14,504
Okay,

355
00:17:14,504 --> 00:17:15,745
first of all,

356
00:17:15,746 --> 00:17:17,385
why don't the programmings have like

357
00:17:17,745 --> 00:17:18,786
.java or something?

358
00:17:19,146 --> 00:17:21,766
Or do they normally have .java or do they not normally have .java or .py or something like that?

359
00:17:24,387 --> 00:17:28,789
So these programs-- I'll get into where we find these programs later and where they're located.

360
00:17:28,989 --> 00:17:31,830
A lot of these are pre-compiled programs that are installed on your computer.

361
00:17:32,130 --> 00:17:33,430
So they're not source files.

362
00:17:34,090 --> 00:17:37,671
They are programs that are built into your computer or that you've installed after the fact.

363
00:17:38,332 --> 00:17:38,832
Okay.

364
00:17:38,912 --> 00:17:39,412
And...

365
00:17:40,496 --> 00:17:42,217
And the strings you write afterwards,

366
00:17:42,477 --> 00:17:46,659
if you write Java with the public static void main string args,

367
00:17:46,759 --> 00:17:48,800
this is the string array?

368
00:17:48,801 --> 00:17:50,780
Right.
So the question here is if you write,

369
00:17:50,781 --> 00:17:51,182
for example,

370
00:17:51,182 --> 00:17:56,723
a Java program and you write a sort of static main function and the args argument you get to that function,

371
00:17:57,003 --> 00:17:59,584
those are exactly what these arguments turn into.

372
00:18:00,464 --> 00:18:01,685
And the same thing for Python.

373
00:18:01,765 --> 00:18:05,508
If you want to access the argv thing that you get in Python,

374
00:18:05,729 --> 00:18:07,550
there is a list of arguments provided through the program.

375
00:18:07,871 --> 00:18:09,432
This is where those arguments come from.

376
00:18:10,773 --> 00:18:15,557
You'll find that you end up running into quoting issues here once you start getting more complicated things.

377
00:18:15,577 --> 00:18:20,702
Like maybe you have a path that has a single quote in the name of a file.

378
00:18:21,062 --> 00:18:23,825
Well now you need to use double quotes around it or you need to escape it.

379
00:18:24,145 --> 00:18:27,308
And escaping is simply,

380
00:18:28,504 --> 00:18:30,125
Putting a backslash in front of a character,

381
00:18:30,365 --> 00:18:32,506
you can do this multiple times to escape multiple characters.

382
00:18:33,227 --> 00:18:35,068
Now you see it has the same effect as though I quoted it.

383
00:18:35,928 --> 00:18:41,611
But doing this once you have a path that includes spaces and also single quotes and also double quotes,

384
00:18:41,971 --> 00:18:48,075
then you might need to get creative with the quoting that you do so that the arguments end up being split in the correct way.

385
00:18:48,915 --> 00:18:50,616
You will run into annoyances around this.

386
00:18:50,656 --> 00:18:57,660
It takes a little while to get used to but there are ways to sanely quote any argument to a command in Bash.

387
00:18:58,876 --> 00:18:59,117
Semi-same.
Yep?

388
00:18:59,117 --> 00:19:05,600
So backslashes separate the same as whitespaces?

389
00:19:05,601 --> 00:19:05,941
No,

390
00:19:05,941 --> 00:19:11,504
backslash is an escape character that says don't treat the next character as special.

391
00:19:12,064 --> 00:19:13,745
So in the case of argument parsing,

392
00:19:14,146 --> 00:19:16,367
backslash space means a literal space.

393
00:19:16,447 --> 00:19:17,808
Do not split on this.

394
00:19:17,848 --> 00:19:19,129
This is not a word boundary.

395
00:19:19,549 --> 00:19:22,050
It is a literal space that's part of an argument.

396
00:19:25,728 --> 00:19:26,228
Cool.

397
00:19:26,388 --> 00:19:26,888
Now,

398
00:19:27,048 --> 00:19:30,970
perhaps the most important command or program you will find to use in...

399
00:19:31,130 --> 00:19:31,771
Yeah,
you had a question?

400
00:19:41,113 --> 00:19:41,573
Yeah,

401
00:19:41,573 --> 00:19:52,156
so if I wanted to say this would not work because it would think that this is one string

402
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:58,014
And then it would say there's a trailing single quote here that doesn't get terminated.

403
00:19:58,415 --> 00:19:59,158
So it just tells me,

404
00:19:59,298 --> 00:20:00,019
oh,
it drops

405
00:20:00,088 --> 00:20:01,509
Back in a continuation prompt,

406
00:20:01,529 --> 00:20:02,709
which is the thing you see at the bottom,

407
00:20:02,729 --> 00:20:04,450
we're just expecting more input.

408
00:20:04,730 --> 00:20:05,750
If I put a single quote here,

409
00:20:06,070 --> 00:20:07,030
then if we get confused,

410
00:20:07,031 --> 00:20:08,051
then all the single quotes are gone.

411
00:20:08,671 --> 00:20:13,793
And so what you could do here is either say this,

412
00:20:14,993 --> 00:20:16,734
or you could double quote the whole string.

413
00:20:18,955 --> 00:20:21,956
And so that way you would end up with the expected output.

414
00:20:23,688 --> 00:20:24,068
Now,

415
00:20:24,068 --> 00:20:26,950
one of the most useful programs you'll find on here is a program called MAN,

416
00:20:27,110 --> 00:20:27,991
short for manual.

417
00:20:28,411 --> 00:20:34,215
The manual allows you to pass in the name of another program and it explains how to use that program.

418
00:20:34,555 --> 00:20:36,136
So for example if I type "man echo",

419
00:20:36,497 --> 00:20:38,018
so echo is the argument here to man,

420
00:20:38,478 --> 00:20:39,759
I get a manual page.

421
00:20:40,159 --> 00:20:42,040
And this says "echo displays a line of text"

422
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:44,562
and then it shows me all of the options,

423
00:20:44,682 --> 00:20:47,264
all of the arguments that I can pass to echo.

424
00:20:48,136 --> 00:20:50,477
And usually some of them will have version information at the bottom,

425
00:20:50,777 --> 00:20:52,478
some of them will also have usage examples.

426
00:20:52,678 --> 00:20:54,139
So if I look up the manual page for date,

427
00:20:54,439 --> 00:20:55,820
you'll see it prints a date.

428
00:20:56,340 --> 00:20:56,860
And it also...

429
00:20:57,541 --> 00:20:58,701
Oh,
this doesn't have that either.

430
00:20:59,241 --> 00:21:00,182
If I do bash maybe.

431
00:21:01,082 --> 00:21:06,445
So the bash one is pretty extensive and it has bug reports and authors and files and restricted...

432
00:21:06,465 --> 00:21:11,507
Like it has a huge manual page that explains all the various things you might want to do in bash.

433
00:21:12,248 --> 00:21:12,768
So the man...

434
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:19,622
program is one you will find you can use a lot in order to understand more about the commands you have and what things they can do.

435
00:21:21,322 --> 00:21:26,904
Usually,
you can get a short version of the manual page for a program by simply passing --help as an argument.

436
00:21:27,464 --> 00:21:28,124
So in this case,

437
00:21:28,604 --> 00:21:30,025
when I run date with --help,

438
00:21:30,205 --> 00:21:32,646
it prints out the help string for date,

439
00:21:32,686 --> 00:21:34,846
which is usually slightly shorter than the man page.

440
00:21:35,446 --> 00:21:38,167
Some commands also allow just -h as a similar thing.

441
00:21:40,368 --> 00:21:40,968
After man

442
00:21:41,208 --> 00:21:44,909
the most important program to know about is cd for Change Directory.

443
00:21:45,689 --> 00:21:49,650
Change Directory allows you to change where you currently are in the file system.

444
00:21:51,211 --> 00:21:52,711
And in this case,

445
00:21:52,951 --> 00:21:54,712
I am currently in my home directory,

446
00:21:55,472 --> 00:21:58,973
and so in my home directory there are a bunch of files,

447
00:21:58,993 --> 00:22:01,594
but if I wanted to operate somewhere else in the file system,

448
00:22:01,814 --> 00:22:04,394
I could change to be in that directory instead.

449
00:22:05,595 --> 00:22:07,175
So what does that look like?

450
00:22:07,695 --> 00:22:08,175
Well,

451
00:22:08,175 --> 00:22:08,896
I type "cd"

452
00:22:09,496 --> 00:22:10,236
for Change Directory,

453
00:22:11,132 --> 00:22:12,233
And then I type a path.

454
00:22:12,693 --> 00:22:13,414
So a path,

455
00:22:13,794 --> 00:22:15,716
in this case it's a path that starts with a slash,

456
00:22:15,756 --> 00:22:17,177
so it makes it an absolute path.

457
00:22:17,237 --> 00:22:18,698
We'll get into other kinds of paths later.

458
00:22:19,178 --> 00:22:23,061
So I want to go into the bin directory under the root of my file system.

459
00:22:23,242 --> 00:22:25,243
So I want to change directory into there.

460
00:22:26,204 --> 00:22:28,586
When I do that you'll see that my prompt has now changed.

461
00:22:28,587 --> 00:22:31,208
It now says I'm in /bin instead of being in /home.

462
00:22:31,868 --> 00:22:34,790
I can also cd directly into the root and so on.

463
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:42,702
You'll find that there are a lot of commands that will operate in the current working directory by default,

464
00:22:42,982 --> 00:22:44,442
unless you tell it something else.

465
00:22:44,722 --> 00:22:47,503
So that's why it is usually useful to know where you are,

466
00:22:47,563 --> 00:22:48,463
hence why it's in the prompt,

467
00:22:48,743 --> 00:22:50,464
and to potentially change where you are.

468
00:22:51,904 --> 00:22:56,265
And it's particularly useful because it allows you to use relative paths.

469
00:22:56,685 --> 00:22:58,026
So here I did

470
00:22:58,506 --> 00:23:01,967
/bin, but imagine that I have some path that's deep under my home directory.

471
00:23:02,207 --> 00:23:02,707
So it's like

472
00:23:03,107 --> 00:23:05,728
/home/john/dev/project/

473
00:23:06,428 --> 00:23:06,928
you know,

474
00:23:08,430 --> 00:23:12,433
6824/version1/ and it becomes a really long thing for me to type all the time.

475
00:23:12,913 --> 00:23:19,319
So instead I can change directory into the folder that I want to be operating in and then use relative paths.

476
00:23:19,939 --> 00:23:23,002
Relative paths are any path that does not start with a slash,

477
00:23:24,263 --> 00:23:29,387
and the resolution for a relative path is in the current working directory,

478
00:23:29,908 --> 00:23:31,749
look up the first path component,

479
00:23:32,009 --> 00:23:35,172
path component or the slash separated parts of the path,

480
00:23:35,584 --> 00:23:36,485
look up the first one,

481
00:23:37,505 --> 00:23:39,686
and then from there traverse using slashes,

482
00:23:39,947 --> 00:23:42,048
rather than starting at the root of the file system.

483
00:23:42,648 --> 00:23:44,209
So for example I'm now in

484
00:23:45,430 --> 00:23:46,590
I can type cd bin,

485
00:23:46,790 --> 00:23:48,191
note the lack of a slash,

486
00:23:48,591 --> 00:23:55,075
to say I want to change directory into the bin folder of what are inside of the current directory.

487
00:23:56,096 --> 00:23:57,336
If I'm now in bin,

488
00:23:57,616 --> 00:24:02,999
and I try to cd to bin again it would tell me there's no such directory because it looks for a folder called bin in

489
00:24:03,580 --> 00:24:04,080
/bin.

490
00:24:05,904 --> 00:24:10,007
If I go back to slash and then try to cd to slash,

491
00:24:10,087 --> 00:24:10,647
well that's fine.

492
00:24:10,648 --> 00:24:11,527
That's an absolute path.

493
00:24:12,208 --> 00:24:16,550
But there are a couple of other special kind of path components you can use in relative paths.

494
00:24:16,610 --> 00:24:19,772
You can use them in absolute paths too but they're more common in relative paths.

495
00:24:20,292 --> 00:24:21,813
Which is dot and dot dot.

496
00:24:22,313 --> 00:24:23,954
So dot is the current directory.

497
00:24:24,775 --> 00:24:30,038
It might seem weird but I can cd into dot and it cds me back into the same place I already was.

498
00:24:30,438 --> 00:24:32,539
So if I cd into bin and then cd dot,

499
00:24:32,799 --> 00:24:33,860
I'm back into slash bin.

500
00:24:34,468 --> 00:24:36,989
So it's just a way to refer to where you currently are.

501
00:24:38,270 --> 00:24:39,110
This can be useful,

502
00:24:39,170 --> 00:24:40,391
we'll see some examples of that later.

503
00:24:41,391 --> 00:24:46,494
The other special path is .dot and .dot refers to the parent directory of wherever we are.

504
00:24:47,014 --> 00:24:48,495
So if I do cd .dot,

505
00:24:48,755 --> 00:24:49,695
I end up in slash.

506
00:24:49,715 --> 00:24:51,596
I end up one level up in my file system.

507
00:24:52,336 --> 00:24:53,737
If I try .dot from slash,

508
00:24:54,157 --> 00:24:55,998
slash like the root of my file system is special,

509
00:24:56,278 --> 00:24:57,859
where .dot just goes back into slash.

510
00:24:58,899 --> 00:24:59,999
But what I can do is I can change

511
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:00,700
So I can say,

512
00:25:00,701 --> 00:25:01,902
I want to go into bin,

513
00:25:02,262 --> 00:25:03,443
and then I want to go back out,

514
00:25:03,764 --> 00:25:05,065
and then I want to go back into bin,

515
00:25:05,466 --> 00:25:07,548
and then I want to go into itself,

516
00:25:07,608 --> 00:25:08,669
and then into itself again,

517
00:25:08,769 --> 00:25:10,030
and then I want to go back to the root,

518
00:25:10,031 --> 00:25:10,851
and then back into bin,

519
00:25:11,111 --> 00:25:12,312
and I end up in /bin.

520
00:25:13,013 --> 00:25:16,997
You can combine these to create paths that navigate the file system however you see fit.

521
00:25:20,428 --> 00:25:21,268
What's the use case of this?

522
00:25:21,909 --> 00:25:23,089
The use case of this is,

523
00:25:23,469 --> 00:25:24,810
imagine you're operating in,

524
00:25:25,850 --> 00:25:26,551
I don't know a,

525
00:25:26,651 --> 00:25:28,692
you're working on a 6824 project.

526
00:25:29,472 --> 00:25:31,213
And now you want to move to working in a,

527
00:25:31,773 --> 00:25:33,494
on a 6858 project.

528
00:25:33,754 --> 00:25:34,134
Well then,

529
00:25:34,134 --> 00:25:34,975
instead of typing,

530
00:25:35,235 --> 00:25:36,455
okay,
I'm in 824,

531
00:25:36,775 --> 00:25:39,296
instead of now having to use an absolute path to say,

532
00:25:39,577 --> 00:25:42,478
now I want to see it into home John dev 6858,

533
00:25:42,798 --> 00:25:47,080
I can just do cd dot dot slash 6858 so go one up

534
00:25:47,468 --> 00:25:49,070
and then one down into a different directory.

535
00:25:49,590 --> 00:25:51,512
This particular path you probably wouldn't use,

536
00:25:51,513 --> 00:25:52,573
because this is pretty useless.

537
00:25:53,394 --> 00:25:56,036
But you don't have to say the same path again.

538
00:25:56,537 --> 00:25:58,979
You could easily-- because once you've done the dot dot,

539
00:25:58,999 --> 00:26:03,564
you are now in-- the reference point is one level up in the fuller hierarchy.

540
00:26:03,565 --> 00:26:04,065
Tilde?

541
00:26:07,067 --> 00:26:07,528
Yes.

542
00:26:07,528 --> 00:26:09,149
So if I do cd tilde,

543
00:26:09,510 --> 00:26:11,231
you'll see I end up back in my home directory.

544
00:26:12,816 --> 00:26:15,158
Slash is the path's component separator.

545
00:26:15,518 --> 00:26:20,523
It is the thing that separates each recursive step of resolving the path.

546
00:26:21,063 --> 00:26:23,485
So if I go to cd a slash b slash d,

547
00:26:24,306 --> 00:26:25,487
a slash b slash c,

548
00:26:26,288 --> 00:26:28,310
what that means is change directory into a,

549
00:26:29,170 --> 00:26:30,051
after doing that,

550
00:26:30,271 --> 00:26:30,832
change it to b,

551
00:26:31,492 --> 00:26:32,133
after doing that,

552
00:26:32,173 --> 00:26:32,713
change it to c.

553
00:26:33,554 --> 00:26:40,060
So that would go into the folder c that's inside the folder b that's inside the folder a.

554
00:26:43,220 --> 00:26:45,843
And these paths also work with tilde,

555
00:26:45,943 --> 00:26:49,988
so I could do tilde slash dot dot and I end up in slash home.

556
00:26:49,989 --> 00:26:54,112
Because it's one directory up from my home directory which is slash home slash john.

557
00:27:01,380 --> 00:27:03,762
There are more efficient ways to navigate the shell as well.

558
00:27:03,763 --> 00:27:04,303
So you'll find,

559
00:27:04,343 --> 00:27:04,804
for example,

560
00:27:04,804 --> 00:27:05,484
in the lecture notes,

561
00:27:05,524 --> 00:27:10,828
there's a reference to a tool called ZOxide that remembers all the paths you've ever CD'd into,

562
00:27:11,209 --> 00:27:16,793
and then gives you a more rapid way to cd into arbitrary of them rather than navigating the whole file system.

563
00:27:17,854 --> 00:27:21,237
So there are tools people have built to more efficiently navigate the shell,

564
00:27:21,458 --> 00:27:23,179
but this will already get you there pretty quickly.

565
00:27:23,419 --> 00:27:26,222
There's one more trick that's useful to know here which is tab.

566
00:27:26,662 --> 00:27:28,704
Tab in Bash gives you autocomplete.

567
00:27:29,452 --> 00:27:30,153
And so for example,

568
00:27:30,173 --> 00:27:33,836
if I do CDB and then press Tab-- well,

569
00:27:33,837 --> 00:27:34,337
in this case,

570
00:27:34,777 --> 00:27:35,537
B is ambiguous.

571
00:27:35,858 --> 00:27:37,119
Oh,
that's because I'm in the wrong place.

572
00:27:37,739 --> 00:27:39,000
CDB and then press Tab.

573
00:27:39,401 --> 00:27:40,922
You'll see if I double-press Tab,

574
00:27:40,923 --> 00:27:42,603
it gives me all the possible options.

575
00:27:43,024 --> 00:27:43,524
In this case,

576
00:27:43,885 --> 00:27:46,066
/bin and /boot are both directories I could go into.

577
00:27:46,447 --> 00:27:46,947
But if I do

578
00:27:47,227 --> 00:27:49,549
CDBI and then press Tab,

579
00:27:49,830 --> 00:27:50,910
it auto completes the bin.

580
00:27:51,471 --> 00:27:54,333
And so this is one way to more rapidly navigate through folders,

581
00:27:54,574 --> 00:27:57,276
is just type the first few letters and then type Tab.

582
00:27:57,728 --> 00:27:58,649
And if those are ambiguous,

583
00:27:58,809 --> 00:28:00,490
TabTab will show you the options.

584
00:28:03,913 --> 00:28:06,556
So what kind of things can we execute in the shell?

585
00:28:06,896 --> 00:28:08,477
So we've already talked about echo,

586
00:28:08,697 --> 00:28:09,158
date,

587
00:28:09,158 --> 00:28:09,918
man,
and CD.

588
00:28:10,579 --> 00:28:12,801
But how do we figure out what else is available to us?

589
00:28:13,341 --> 00:28:13,841
And in fact,

590
00:28:13,902 --> 00:28:17,665
how does the shell even know when I type date which program to run?

591
00:28:18,045 --> 00:28:19,827
Like where is date on my computer?

592
00:28:21,428 --> 00:28:26,091
The reality is that when you type a command with a program name into your shell,

593
00:28:26,812 --> 00:28:32,096
what it would actually do is it will take the name of the program and it will look it up in something called "path".

594
00:28:32,736 --> 00:28:35,478
Path is an environment variable.

595
00:28:35,818 --> 00:28:41,502
So it's a variable that's sort of set across my whole shell of variable to value mappings,

596
00:28:41,602 --> 00:28:43,404
usually names to strings.

597
00:28:44,284 --> 00:28:49,268
And those will describe a bunch of meta information that the shell can make use of like

598
00:28:49,468 --> 00:28:50,348
What user am I?

599
00:28:50,408 --> 00:28:52,049
What host is this?

600
00:28:52,309 --> 00:28:53,370
What shell is being used?

601
00:28:53,390 --> 00:28:53,950
That kind of stuff.

602
00:28:54,450 --> 00:28:55,051
And path,

603
00:28:55,151 --> 00:28:56,511
and here we can use the echo tool again.

604
00:28:56,831 --> 00:28:58,012
If I echo my path,

605
00:28:58,532 --> 00:29:00,853
it will list out a sequence of paths.

606
00:29:01,173 --> 00:29:02,474
They're colon separated here.

607
00:29:03,214 --> 00:29:05,995
And when you try to run a program in your shell,

608
00:29:06,516 --> 00:29:12,818
it will walk through these folders one by one looking for a file by the name of the program.

609
00:29:13,459 --> 00:29:14,219
And if it finds it,

610
00:29:14,299 --> 00:29:14,759
run it.

611
00:29:14,759 --> 00:29:15,780
Otherwise look in the next one.

612
00:29:16,788 --> 00:29:18,529
And we can actually introspect this.

613
00:29:18,789 --> 00:29:20,210
If I run which date,

614
00:29:20,531 --> 00:29:27,856
so which is a program that will walk through the path list and print out the place where it first finds a given program,

615
00:29:28,356 --> 00:29:32,619
it will say that the location of my date command is at this path.

616
00:29:33,399 --> 00:29:34,720
So when I run date,

617
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:43,066
what actually happens is my shell discovers that path slash run slash current system slash sw bin date and then runs that program,

618
00:29:43,146 --> 00:29:44,207
executes that program,

619
00:29:44,567 --> 00:29:46,028
and that's what ends up in my terminal.

620
00:29:46,444 --> 00:29:49,347
And this is so that we don't have to write the full paths every time.

621
00:29:49,688 --> 00:29:50,449
So I could,

622
00:29:50,509 --> 00:29:51,250
if I wanted to,

623
00:29:52,231 --> 00:29:55,555
take this path and have that be my program name,

624
00:29:56,176 --> 00:29:57,337
and that will run just the same.

625
00:29:57,797 --> 00:30:00,000
But it's more convenient for the shell to be able to do that.

626
00:30:00,020 --> 00:30:01,162
discover them in this way.

627
00:30:01,722 --> 00:30:02,864
Which is also just a program,

628
00:30:02,884 --> 00:30:03,825
so I can run which,

629
00:30:04,206 --> 00:30:04,706
which,

630
00:30:04,827 --> 00:30:08,852
and it tells me that the which program is located in the same folder as date was.

631
00:30:09,833 --> 00:30:11,616
This path might be different on your host,

632
00:30:11,617 --> 00:30:15,181
it just depends on where software is installed on your particular operating system.

633
00:30:16,062 --> 00:30:16,683
Do you have a question?

634
00:30:18,245 --> 00:30:18,985
Okay,

635
00:30:19,646 --> 00:30:25,310
so when you type it non-completely,

636
00:30:26,110 --> 00:30:28,692
it searches in folders that--it searches in the parents of your current directory?

637
00:30:29,293 --> 00:30:29,653
No.

638
00:30:29,653 --> 00:30:32,555
It searches in the folders listed in my path.

639
00:30:34,897 --> 00:30:44,223
So the path variable is sort of a special variable that's known by my shell and it will search through these directories in order for the program by the name that I gave.

640
00:30:46,824 --> 00:30:54,589
If you have multiple programs of the same name in more than one folder that's in the path,

641
00:30:54,590 --> 00:30:56,270
how does it choose which one to use?

642
00:30:56,271 --> 00:31:00,432
So if a particular program is present in multiple paths in my path,

643
00:31:00,592 --> 00:31:01,392
like $PATH,

644
00:31:02,373 --> 00:31:06,275
my shell will execute the first of them that it encounters in the path order.

645
00:31:07,436 --> 00:31:09,417
And you can even ask which with the

646
00:31:09,757 --> 00:31:10,337
-a flag,

647
00:31:10,357 --> 00:31:11,278
which is for all,

648
00:31:12,118 --> 00:31:14,600
I can ask which all the copies I have of echo.

649
00:31:15,116 --> 00:31:16,797
And it will say there's only one echo for you.

650
00:31:17,597 --> 00:31:19,498
If I try to do something else like rustc,

651
00:31:19,518 --> 00:31:20,418
well,

652
00:31:20,419 --> 00:31:21,118
there's also only one.

653
00:31:21,358 --> 00:31:22,899
Do I have anything that would be multiple of?

654
00:31:23,499 --> 00:31:23,999
Mmm,

655
00:31:27,820 --> 00:31:28,320
SH.

656
00:31:29,401 --> 00:31:34,523
You'll see that there are actually two places in my path where SH appears and it will show all of them.

657
00:31:35,243 --> 00:31:37,644
But your shell will normally execute the first one only.

658
00:31:39,993 --> 00:31:41,013
What's the minus a for?

659
00:31:41,014 --> 00:31:42,974
It's an argument to which that lets it,

660
00:31:43,234 --> 00:31:46,456
that makes it print all of the paths rather than just the one it would have selected.

661
00:31:46,876 --> 00:31:48,537
And in fact if I tap man which,

662
00:31:49,638 --> 00:31:56,902
you'll see that there's under options it says dash a or dash dash all prints all matching executables in path not just the first.

663
00:32:05,344 --> 00:32:10,528
And this also gives us a clue for how we can figure out what all of the programs we have are.

664
00:32:10,989 --> 00:32:11,369
Right?

665
00:32:11,369 --> 00:32:14,392
Is that you could walk through all the directories in your path,

666
00:32:14,832 --> 00:32:17,915
and then just list the contents of all of them.

667
00:32:18,115 --> 00:32:19,516
And that is all of my programs.

668
00:32:19,836 --> 00:32:20,897
You might not know what all of them do,

669
00:32:21,077 --> 00:32:23,679
but you can do that by looking up the man pages and then explore.

670
00:32:23,700 --> 00:32:25,421
So if you really want to go through this process...

671
00:32:25,681 --> 00:32:26,262
So in my case,

672
00:32:26,263 --> 00:32:26,763
for example,

673
00:32:27,202 --> 00:32:31,626
I could take this directory and do ls and this path.

674
00:32:32,287 --> 00:32:33,528
ls is another program

675
00:32:33,828 --> 00:32:35,009
that is the list program.

676
00:32:35,069 --> 00:32:37,010
It lists the contents of a directory.

677
00:32:37,610 --> 00:32:38,130
So in this case,

678
00:32:38,150 --> 00:32:38,931
I'm telling list,

679
00:32:39,211 --> 00:32:39,631
"Hey,

680
00:32:39,631 --> 00:32:42,032
can you go list the contents of that directory?"

681
00:32:42,372 --> 00:32:42,872
And if I do,

682
00:32:43,133 --> 00:32:45,474
it prints out a long list of all the file names written there.

683
00:32:45,954 --> 00:32:48,736
And these are all programs that I could run on my command line.

684
00:32:49,956 --> 00:32:54,598
One thing worth noting here is if I type ls and don't give it an argument,

685
00:32:54,999 --> 00:32:56,940
then it will list the current directory.

686
00:32:58,124 --> 00:33:05,048
And this is what I mean by the current working directory is the thing you change with cd is often the one you want to make use of.

687
00:33:05,588 --> 00:33:08,890
Because that is where many commands will operate by default.

688
00:33:11,712 --> 00:33:14,895
There are a bunch of other programs that are useful to know about like this.

689
00:33:14,935 --> 00:33:15,755
So for example,

690
00:33:16,056 --> 00:33:17,617
there is one called cat.

691
00:33:18,137 --> 00:33:20,819
Cat will print out the contents of a file.

692
00:33:21,400 --> 00:33:22,861
So if I do cat of

693
00:33:24,182 --> 00:33:27,305
Stata.config, then when I cat that file,

694
00:33:27,485 --> 00:33:30,727
it prints out the contents of that file and dumps them into my terminal.

695
00:33:32,533 --> 00:33:34,154
There are other commands like sort.

696
00:33:34,854 --> 00:33:39,378
Sort will take its input or the file that is referred to on its input,

697
00:33:40,058 --> 00:33:42,420
and it will print out the lines in sorted order.

698
00:33:42,900 --> 00:33:54,569
So if for example here I create a data file and I put in A3BX14 and I type "sort data",

699
00:33:55,149 --> 00:33:57,011
then it will print those out in sorted order.

700
00:33:58,712 --> 00:33:59,212
And in fact,

701
00:33:59,613 --> 00:34:00,934
if I do here-- let's say

702
00:34:02,536 --> 00:34:05,398
I add 31 as well to prove a point.

703
00:34:05,979 --> 00:34:09,162
You'll see that it does lexiographical sorting by default.

704
00:34:09,542 --> 00:34:10,002
So

705
00:34:10,002 --> 00:34:12,985
3 is followed by 31 because alphabetically,

706
00:34:13,165 --> 00:34:13,806
that is the order.

707
00:34:13,906 --> 00:34:15,267
Even numerically,

708
00:34:15,407 --> 00:34:16,248
that might not be correct.

709
00:34:16,488 --> 00:34:17,009
For example,

710
00:34:17,269 --> 00:34:19,811
if I wanted to add in now-- no.

711
00:34:21,233 --> 00:34:24,115
If I wanted to add in 4 and I tried to sort data,

712
00:34:24,900 --> 00:34:25,960
then you'll see that I get 3,

713
00:34:26,061 --> 00:34:26,521
31,

714
00:34:26,521 --> 00:34:29,182
4 because they're ordered lexicographically not numerically.

715
00:34:29,542 --> 00:34:31,923
But there are arguments you can pass to sort to change this behavior.

716
00:34:32,983 --> 00:34:33,784
Then we have unique.

717
00:34:34,704 --> 00:34:38,266
So if I here added four a bunch of times,

718
00:34:39,886 --> 00:34:41,047
then if I now run--

719
00:34:45,248 --> 00:34:46,809
If I now run unique on data,

720
00:34:47,130 --> 00:34:50,032
it will only print out lines that are unique in that file.

721
00:34:50,592 --> 00:34:53,255
It only eliminates consecutive lines that are unique though.

722
00:34:53,635 --> 00:34:58,339
So if I go into data and I put a 3 in here and type unique data,

723
00:34:58,439 --> 00:34:59,239
you'll see that I get 4,

724
00:34:59,299 --> 00:35:00,040
3,
4.

725
00:35:00,576 --> 00:35:01,557
So it's not very smart.

726
00:35:01,558 --> 00:35:03,198
It doesn't remember every line it's ever seen.

727
00:35:03,578 --> 00:35:06,400
It only eliminates duplicates that are consecutive.

728
00:35:06,921 --> 00:35:11,464
But you can imagine combining sort and unique to get the functionality you want.

729
00:35:12,224 --> 00:35:12,724
Or,

730
00:35:12,965 --> 00:35:14,286
sort also has a flag,

731
00:35:14,566 --> 00:35:15,266
sort-u,

732
00:35:15,747 --> 00:35:16,527
which does this for you.

733
00:35:16,567 --> 00:35:18,349
It sorts and eliminates duplicates.

734
00:35:20,190 --> 00:35:22,471
But we'll show you how to combine commands a little bit later.

735
00:35:23,532 --> 00:35:24,873
And then we have head and tail.

736
00:35:25,954 --> 00:35:28,676
Head prints the first n lines of a file.

737
00:35:30,408 --> 00:35:31,650
The default n here is 10,

738
00:35:31,810 --> 00:35:36,676
but you can pass -n and then a numeric argument to say how many lines to include.

739
00:35:36,976 --> 00:35:38,958
So head prints from the start of the file,

740
00:35:39,639 --> 00:35:42,663
and then we also have tail which prints from the end of the file.

741
00:35:43,483 --> 00:35:46,587
So these allow you to inspect just parts of a file rather than the full file.

742
00:35:49,253 --> 00:35:51,555
And then we get into slightly more sophisticated tools.

743
00:35:51,735 --> 00:35:52,836
So there for example,

744
00:35:53,037 --> 00:35:53,978
there's a tool called grep.

745
00:35:54,678 --> 00:35:58,262
Grep is a file searcher.

746
00:35:58,943 --> 00:36:00,324
It doesn't search for files,

747
00:36:00,344 --> 00:36:04,729
it searches in files and it searches for things that match a particular pattern.

748
00:36:05,129 --> 00:36:07,372
So for example if I want to grep for

749
00:36:09,025 --> 00:36:10,766
4 or 3 is maybe a better example.

