00:49:09,499 --> 00:49:14,544
-l means print the name of the file that matched rather than the line that matched.

997
00:49:15,384 --> 00:49:22,931
So this will tell me all of the markdown files in the current directory where todo is written in the file somewhere.

998
00:49:24,644 --> 00:49:26,305
So this can be useful if you're programming,

999
00:49:26,306 --> 00:49:26,726
and you're like,

1000
00:49:26,726 --> 00:49:28,567
oh,
which things did I mean to get back to,

1001
00:49:28,568 --> 00:49:29,368
but I never looked for them?

1002
00:49:31,649 --> 00:49:35,092
This particular one might seem very similar to me doing this.

1003
00:49:35,832 --> 00:49:37,654
There's grep for todo in the current directory,

1004
00:49:37,914 --> 00:49:39,595
but the difference is here I got to say,

1005
00:49:40,055 --> 00:49:42,597
find me only markdown files and only do it for those.

1006
00:49:43,438 --> 00:49:43,938
But again,

1007
00:49:43,978 --> 00:49:46,120
you can make these as complicated as you want to be.

1008
00:49:47,121 --> 00:49:47,641
And with find,

1009
00:49:47,661 --> 00:49:50,863
of course,
you can add a bunch more pattern matching to this if you wish.

1010
00:49:53,406 --> 00:49:53,707
Now,

1011
00:49:53,707 --> 00:49:55,871
the syntax of find can be pretty weird.

1012
00:49:56,212 --> 00:49:59,299
I'm not expecting you to learn find just from seeing these few examples.

1013
00:50:00,148 --> 00:50:01,169
Find will tell you a lot.

1014
00:50:01,510 --> 00:50:13,844
This is also the kind of thing that it's really helpful actually to ask LLMs because there's a lot of examples online of writing find command lines and LLMs can usually spit out a thing that will do roughly the thing you asked for pretty easily.

1015
00:50:14,345 --> 00:50:19,651
There are also more modern tools that are more ergonomic and harder to misuse like FD that are linked in the lecture notes.

