00:51:23,975 --> 00:51:24,396
Awk,

1038
00:51:24,396 --> 00:51:24,996
like sed,

1039
00:51:25,136 --> 00:51:27,798
is a program that has its own programming language built in.

1040
00:51:28,558 --> 00:51:30,840
But where sed is for editing files,

1041
00:51:31,300 --> 00:51:33,482
awk is for parsing files.

1042
00:51:34,242 --> 00:51:35,803
Awk is a tool that will,

1043
00:51:36,204 --> 00:51:36,964
by default,

1044
00:51:37,664 --> 00:51:40,446
split a file by whitespace and by lines,

1045
00:51:41,167 --> 00:51:45,630
and then let you write expressions over the result of parsing in that way.

1046
00:51:46,210 --> 00:51:48,612
So if we go back to our home directory here,

1047
00:51:49,132 --> 00:51:50,512
where we have our data file.

1048
00:51:50,513 --> 00:51:52,913
Let's go back and clear out that file,

1049
00:51:53,193 --> 00:51:55,994
and I will do A B C D E F G

1050
00:51:56,474 --> 00:51:57,514
H I matrix,

1051
00:51:57,654 --> 00:51:58,155
good old matrix.

1052
00:51:59,055 --> 00:52:02,296
And then I write awk print two of data.

1053
00:52:03,876 --> 00:52:06,557
So the thing again in the quoted string is an awk program.

1054
00:52:06,797 --> 00:52:09,678
We're not gonna teach you the awk programming language here 'cause it's fairly extensive,

1055
00:52:09,898 --> 00:52:14,599
but you can often do very simple things by just learning a couple of key bits about awk.

1056
00:52:15,259 --> 00:52:16,139
So this is,

1057
00:52:16,680 --> 00:52:18,140
the curly bracket here indicates

1058
00:52:18,616 --> 00:52:19,196
run this,

1059
00:52:19,476 --> 00:52:23,298
that's sort of the way to express a run and execute block in awk.

1060
00:52:23,878 --> 00:52:28,420
And you can write a pattern before the curly brackets here to say

1061
00:52:28,760 --> 00:52:35,663
I want to only run this expression on lines that have certain properties but in this case because I didn't specify a pattern it's all lines.

1062
00:52:36,984 --> 00:52:38,344
And for every line

1063
00:52:38,684 --> 00:52:41,325
I want to print the second field.

1064
00:52:42,086 --> 00:52:47,068
So awk will parse the file into records so rows and fields which are columns

1065
00:52:47,408 --> 00:52:49,790
And so in this case I say print out the second field,

1066
00:52:50,270 --> 00:52:52,472
and the default field separator is whitespace.

1067
00:52:52,792 --> 00:52:56,234
So if I do this it will print out only the second column of every row.

1068
00:52:58,396 --> 00:53:00,017
But I can change what the field separator is.

1069
00:53:00,378 --> 00:53:02,159
So let's say I switch this to be comma.

1070
00:53:02,559 --> 00:53:04,541
Well this just became a CSV parser.

1071
00:53:04,542 --> 00:53:08,824
Because now it splits lines by commas into columns,

1072
00:53:08,924 --> 00:53:11,225
and the lines are records,

1073
00:53:11,506 --> 00:53:14,448
and the columns are colon-separated columns.

1074
00:53:14,688 --> 00:53:15,469
that you can then print out.

1075
00:53:15,709 --> 00:53:16,249
If I do this,

1076
00:53:16,490 --> 00:53:17,951
there is no second column anymore,

1077
00:53:18,231 --> 00:53:19,652
because everything is in the first column,

1078
00:53:19,653 --> 00:53:22,034
because there are no commas in this file.

1079
00:53:25,037 --> 00:53:26,899
I can also do things like-- in fact,

1080
00:53:27,479 --> 00:53:31,483
I'll show you a more complicated awk expression later for the kinds of things it can do.

1081
00:53:32,323 --> 00:53:36,647
But awk is very useful tool for just pulling data out of semi-structured files very easily.

1082
00:53:38,869 --> 00:53:39,369
Now,

1083
