1096
00:54:12,757 --> 00:54:13,537
That's -b

1097
00:54:14,298 --> 00:54:14,798
-1.

1098
00:54:15,698 --> 00:54:16,699
And then it will grep,

1099
00:54:17,019 --> 00:54:17,819
so search for,

1100
00:54:18,079 --> 00:54:19,580
the string disconnected from.

1101
00:54:21,068 --> 00:54:23,130
It will take that and it will parse it to sed.

1102
00:54:24,050 --> 00:54:27,773
Sed is going to replace in all the lines,

1103
00:54:28,233 --> 00:54:32,896
this regular expression with that backslash one which is a back reference,

1104
00:54:32,916 --> 00:54:34,518
which is another thing you can do with regular expressions,

1105
00:54:35,358 --> 00:54:42,163
which references the parentheses enclosed expression in the search string.

1106
00:54:42,843 --> 00:54:46,386
So the backslash one here ends up referring to whatever this matched.

1107
00:54:46,926 --> 00:54:49,028
This is the kind of cool search replace you can do with sed.

1108
00:54:50,025 --> 00:54:51,848
So in this case it strips out,

1109
00:54:52,028 --> 00:54:58,898
the way to read this expression is any number of characters followed by the string disconnected from followed by a space,

1110
00:54:59,179 --> 00:55:00,000
followed by any number of

1111
00:55:00,060 --> 00:55:01,401
characters and a space,

1112
00:55:01,541 --> 00:55:02,121
then user,

1113
00:55:02,201 --> 00:55:02,781
then a space,

1114
00:55:03,081 --> 00:55:04,142
then any number of characters,

1115
00:55:04,182 --> 00:55:04,782
then a space,

1116
00:55:05,202 --> 00:55:07,663
then anything that's not a space,

1117
00:55:08,283 --> 00:55:08,924
then space,

1118
00:55:09,144 --> 00:55:09,724
then port,

1119
00:55:10,004 --> 00:55:10,504
and so on.

