1127
00:55:34,926 --> 00:55:37,207
Then I sort all of those results.

1128
00:55:37,708 --> 00:55:40,789
Then I pick up the unique lines from that sorted result.

1129
00:55:41,329 --> 00:55:41,789
The

1130
00:55:41,789 --> 00:55:46,211
-c argument to unique prints the count of any that were duplicates.

1131
00:55:46,651 --> 00:55:49,713
So if the word John appears three times in a row,

1132
00:55:49,973 --> 00:55:52,314
it will print John 3 instead of John John John,

1133
00:55:52,554 --> 00:55:53,354
or instead of just John.

1134
00:55:53,634 --> 00:55:54,535
So it gives me the count.

1135
00:55:55,435 --> 00:55:57,096
Then I sort that output

1136
00:55:57,712 --> 00:55:58,353
numerically

1137
00:55:58,813 --> 00:56:02,015
-n using the first column,

1138
00:56:02,016 --> 00:56:03,997
so the column that contains the count.

1139
00:56:04,737 --> 00:56:14,204
So now I have all of the usernames that we use to log in with their counts in sorted order such that the most common ones are at the bottom.

1140
00:56:15,545 --> 00:56:20,569
And then I use tail-n 10 to get the last 10 which are the top 10.

1141
00:56:21,349 --> 00:56:24,671
Then I use awk to print out only the username and not the count.

1142
00:56:25,880 --> 00:56:29,542
And then I paste all those lines together into a single line,

1143
00:56:29,602 --> 00:56:30,102
dash s,

1144
00:56:30,542 --> 00:56:31,663
that are comma separated,

1145
00:56:31,923 --> 00:56:32,843
which is the d comma.

1146
00:56:34,104 --> 00:56:35,604
And so this command when I run it,

1147
00:56:35,945 --> 00:56:36,725
if it works correctly,

1148
