1187
00:58:54,012 --> 00:58:56,893
So this shows you the kind of thing that you can do with bash,

1188
00:58:56,894 --> 00:59:01,515
is that you can write decently complex expressions to sort of tie all of this stuff together.

1189
00:59:02,175 --> 00:59:05,736
Now one of the things you'll recognize here is that I introduced a new concept:

1190
00:59:06,056 --> 00:59:07,317
this vertical bar,

1191
00:59:07,397 --> 00:59:08,437
the pipe character.

1192
00:59:09,137 --> 00:59:17,580
This is this is a construct of the bash programming language and what the pipe character does is it says run the left program and

1193
00:59:18,136 --> 00:59:22,937
take its output and make that be the input of the program to the right of the pipe.

1194
00:59:23,758 --> 00:59:25,518
So it's a way to connect programs,

1195
00:59:26,078 --> 00:59:30,279
so that instead of the input being your keyboard and the output being your terminal,

1196
00:59:30,559 --> 00:59:33,460
the input is some other program or the output is some other programs.

1197
00:59:34,901 --> 00:59:37,481
There are a bunch of these kinds of operators in bash.

1198
00:59:37,821 --> 00:59:38,622
So for example,

1199
00:59:39,242 --> 00:59:40,722
you know I ran the date command.

1200
00:59:40,982 --> 00:59:41,682
Well I can say

1201
00:59:42,423 --> 00:59:44,923
I want to take the date and I want to write it into...

1202
00:59:48,120 --> 00:59:49,182
the date.text.

1203
00:59:49,583 --> 00:59:55,553
So the angle bracket here to the right is saying take the output of this command and write it into this file instead of to my terminal.

1204
00:59:56,274 --> 00:59:57,336
And now if I cat,

1205
00:59:57,476 --> 00:59:58,778
so print out the contents of this file,

1206
00:59:58,958 --> 00:59:59,940
you'll see that that was the result.

1207
01:00:00,068 --> 01:00:01,710
of date.

1208
01:00:01,711 --> 01:00:02,211
Similarly,

1209
01:00:02,450 --> 01:00:07,595
you can use the angle bracket in the other direction to say instead of taking input from my terminal,

1210
01:00:07,975 --> 01:00:10,437
take the input from that location.

1211
01:00:10,898 --> 01:00:12,119
So I can for cat,

1212
01:00:12,139 --> 01:00:12,639
for example,

1213
01:00:12,699 --> 01:00:16,563
say the input for cat is going to be the data text.

1214
01:00:19,786 --> 01:00:20,246
Similarly,

1215
01:00:20,246 --> 01:00:21,307
I could do for sort.

1216
01:00:22,444 --> 01:00:23,045
The date.txt,

1217
01:00:23,105 --> 01:00:23,925
although for sort,

1218
01:00:24,106 --> 01:00:24,726
when there's one line,

1219
01:00:24,727 --> 01:00:25,587
there's nothing that happens.

1220
01:00:25,987 --> 01:00:28,570
But this allows me to redirect the input and output of programs.

1221
01:00:29,010 --> 01:00:29,550
This can be useful,

1222
01:00:29,571 --> 01:00:29,991
for example,

1223
01:00:29,991 --> 01:00:33,554
to put something in a file so that you can look at it later.

1224
01:00:33,794 --> 01:00:35,035
And you can walk away from your computer,

1225
01:00:35,075 --> 01:00:36,897
and even if it scrolled far back,

1226
01:00:36,937 --> 01:00:37,978
you still have it in a file.

1227
01:00:40,456 --> 01:00:45,980
One thing to be aware of for the angle bracket to the right is that it overwrites the file.

1228
01:00:46,440 --> 01:00:48,081
So if I now cap the date,

1229
01:00:48,121 --> 01:00:49,722
you'll see it only has one date entry.

1230
01:00:49,882 --> 01:00:50,623
If I run it again,

1231
01:00:50,663 --> 01:00:51,823
it only has one date entry.

1232
01:00:52,924 --> 01:00:56,726
You can use two of them to say append to the file instead of replacing it.

1233
01:00:57,547 --> 01:00:58,988
And so now it has two lines instead of one.

1234
01:01:00,808 --> 01:01:02,330
There's a whole programming language here.

1235
01:01:02,450 --> 01:01:05,372
And going into all of what bash means,

1236
01:01:05,772 --> 01:01:06,673
we're not gonna have time for.

1237
01:01:07,434 --> 01:01:08,514
But let me give you very,

1238
01:01:08,634 --> 01:01:09,615
very quick recap.

1239
01:01:10,576 --> 01:01:13,838
So bash has conditionals.

1240
01:01:14,139 --> 01:01:16,500
So you can write things like if grep

1241
01:01:18,462 --> 01:01:20,363
2026, the date.txt,

1242
01:01:20,383 --> 01:01:25,587
then echo its 2026 fee.

1243
01:01:26,900 --> 01:01:28,942
So this will run the grep program,

1244
01:01:29,403 --> 01:01:32,206
and if the grep program exited successfully,

1245
01:01:32,987 --> 01:01:36,370
then and only then does it run the command that's after "then"

1246
01:01:36,490 --> 01:01:36,990
here.

1247
01:01:37,071 --> 01:01:39,353
And I could have multiple commands here if I wanted to.

1248
01:01:40,334 --> 01:01:40,834
Hello

1249
01:01:42,316 --> 01:01:45,239
And it will execute all the programs that are in the "then"

1250
01:01:45,720 --> 01:01:46,220
branch,

1251
01:01:46,841 --> 01:01:48,823
only if the program exited with a success.

1252
01:01:49,764 --> 01:01:52,186
And you'll see the syntax here is kind of archaic,

1253
01:01:52,226 --> 01:01:53,487
there's a bunch of semicolons and stuff.

1254
01:01:53,967 --> 01:01:56,369
LLMs are very good at writing these kinds of commands if you want to.

1255
01:01:57,210 --> 01:01:59,992
Humans less so unless you've done it a bunch of times.

1256
01:02:00,312 --> 01:02:02,053
You'll see for example that the way to end an "if"

1257
01:02:02,153 --> 01:02:02,914
is with "fi".

1258
01:02:03,294 --> 01:02:04,595
Because why not?

1259
01:02:05,596 --> 01:02:10,519
If you wonder how did I know that grep exits successfully when it finds something,

1260
01:02:10,820 --> 01:02:12,821
this is something that's also in the grep manual.

1261
01:02:13,241 --> 01:02:14,963
So usually in most of the grep manuals,

1262
01:02:15,263 --> 01:02:16,764
if you search for exit status

1263
01:02:16,984 --> 01:02:19,586
you'll find an entry that explains what this program exits with.

1264
01:02:20,047 --> 01:02:20,927
So in the case of grep,

1265
01:02:21,228 --> 01:02:23,650
it exits with zero if a line is selected,

1266
01:02:23,890 --> 01:02:24,750
zero is success,

1267
01:02:25,211 --> 01:02:29,514
and it exits with one if no lines were selected and two if an error occurred.

1268
01:02:30,175 --> 01:02:30,675
And in general,

1269
01:02:30,715 --> 01:02:34,198
zero is success and any non-zero exit status is not a success.

1270
01:02:36,080 --> 01:02:37,001
Then there are while loops.

1271
01:02:37,241 --> 01:02:39,042
So you can say while grep 2026,

1272
01:02:39,743 --> 01:02:41,925
the date dot text,

1273
01:02:42,525 --> 01:02:43,025
then echo,

1274
01:02:44,447 --> 01:02:45,528
it's still 2026.

1275
01:02:46,776 --> 01:02:50,417
and then write the date to the date.text,

1276
01:02:51,618 --> 01:02:52,758
and then done.

1277
01:03:00,620 --> 01:03:01,921
So this is a while loop.

1278
01:03:02,261 --> 01:03:05,962
This will run this loop as long as this command remains true.

1279
01:03:06,922 --> 01:03:11,724
So this would continue to print excessively into my terminal until it was no longer 2026.

1280
01:03:12,792 --> 01:03:13,453
But I can inject,

1281
01:03:13,454 --> 01:03:13,893
for example,

1282
01:03:13,893 --> 01:03:14,713
here sleep 10,

1283
01:03:14,774 --> 01:03:15,994
which is sleep for 10 seconds.

1284
01:03:16,255 --> 01:03:17,235
And then it will run it once.

1285
01:03:17,616 --> 01:03:19,317
And when it gets to running sleep,

1286
01:03:19,577 --> 01:03:20,538
it will wait for 10 seconds.

1287
01:03:20,598 --> 01:03:21,839
And then we'll run the command again,

1288
01:03:22,119 --> 01:03:22,980
see if it remains true,

1289
01:03:23,220 --> 01:03:24,561
and keep iterating through like this.

1290
01:03:24,821 --> 01:03:25,842
So after about 10 seconds,

1291
01:03:25,843 --> 01:03:26,622
we should see print again.

1292
01:03:26,642 --> 01:03:27,142
There we go.

1293
01:03:28,744 --> 01:03:29,644
There are also for loops.

1294
01:03:30,065 --> 01:03:34,688
For loops are of the form for variable name in,

1295
01:03:35,189 --> 01:03:39,532
and then a white space separated list of words.

1296
01:03:40,076 --> 01:03:42,598
where the for loop will be executed once per word,

1297
01:03:45,440 --> 01:03:51,985
and a variable will be set by the name that you specified in the for loop on each iteration.

1298
01:03:53,906 --> 01:03:57,128
So in this case it will run once with var_name set to A,

1299
01:03:57,589 --> 01:03:58,610
once with var_name set to B,

1300
01:03:58,850 --> 01:03:59,750
once with var_name set to C,

1301
01:03:59,810 --> 01:04:00,310
and so on.

1302
01:04:00,631 --> 01:04:04,113
If you're wondering "well how do I turn this into one of the for loops that I know that iterate over numbers?"

1303
01:04:04,654 --> 01:04:05,154
Well,

1304
01:04:05,274 --> 01:04:07,236
you can use here a little trick.

1305
01:04:08,640 --> 01:04:15,045
So if you use $ it's a way in Bash to say run the program inside the parentheses,

1306
01:04:15,525 --> 01:04:18,608
then replace this whole thing with the output of that program.

1307
01:04:19,969 --> 01:04:20,629
Why does this work?

1308
01:04:20,989 --> 01:04:21,489
Well,

1309
01:04:21,670 --> 01:04:24,872
the seek command prints out numbers.

1310
01:04:25,473 --> 01:04:26,354
If I say 1-10,

1311
01:04:26,754 --> 01:04:29,576
it will print all the numbers from 1-10 in increments of 1.

1312
01:04:30,517 --> 01:04:31,157
I could also say

1313
01:04:31,558 --> 01:04:33,459
2 to print them in increments of...

1314
01:04:35,116 --> 01:04:35,616
2,

1315
01:04:35,757 --> 01:04:36,337
if I wanted to.

1316
01:04:36,718 --> 01:04:39,160
But normally you would do something like seek 110.

1317
01:04:39,520 --> 01:04:49,930
This then gets substituted for the seek expression in this line and then gets whitespace separated into the tokens or the words 1,

1318
01:04:50,010 --> 01:04:50,551
2,
3,

1319
01:04:50,551 --> 01:04:50,972
4,
5,

1320
01:04:50,972 --> 01:04:51,472
and so on.

1321
01:04:51,632 --> 01:04:55,155
So var name gets set to each of those values on each iteration of the for loop.

1322
01:04:55,156 --> 01:04:55,676
Yep?

1323
01:04:56,617 --> 01:04:58,338
Does that mean you can replace the pipe operator with that?

1324
01:04:58,339 --> 01:04:59,339
If you just nest them,

1325
01:04:59,359 --> 01:04:59,859
nest two for loops?

1326
01:05:02,949 --> 01:05:05,370
So you couldn't quite replace the pipe operator with this,

1327
01:05:05,630 --> 01:05:07,571
because this turns it into an argument.

1328
01:05:08,391 --> 01:05:09,392
Whereas the sort command,

1329
01:05:09,412 --> 01:05:09,972
for example,

1330
01:05:10,252 --> 01:05:12,053
takes its input on input,

1331
01:05:12,273 --> 01:05:13,814
not as an argument.

1332
01:05:13,815 --> 01:05:15,314
It doesn't sort its arguments,

1333
01:05:15,634 --> 01:05:17,435
it sorts the contents of its argument.

1334
01:05:18,916 --> 01:05:20,997
But there are ways to do that as well,

1335
01:05:21,757 --> 01:05:23,077
but I'm not going to talk about them here,

1336
01:05:23,097 --> 01:05:24,038
because we don't have the time.

1337
01:05:25,458 --> 01:05:29,400
But some of the lecture exercises go into some of those kinds of things that you could do.

1338
01:05:31,861 --> 01:05:32,361
Now,

1339
01:05:33,142 --> 01:05:35,104
there are two last things I want to tell you about.

1340
01:05:36,345 --> 01:05:39,047
One of them is when you write if expressions,

1341
01:05:40,468 --> 01:05:41,069
you might wonder,

1342
01:05:41,149 --> 01:05:41,929
well,
okay,

1343
01:05:42,009 --> 01:05:43,270
I can do things like grep and stuff,

1344
01:05:43,310 --> 01:05:45,412
but what if I just want to compare variables?

1345
01:05:45,812 --> 01:05:46,613
Like,
what if I just want to do,

1346
01:05:46,693 --> 01:05:47,193
like,

1347
01:05:47,254 --> 01:05:48,074
numerical comparison,

1348
01:05:48,114 --> 01:05:51,357
like,
things that I normally do with ifs that are not related to command?

1349
01:05:51,897 --> 01:05:53,218
Well,
there's a program called test.

1350
01:05:53,739 --> 01:05:54,700
The test program,

1351
01:05:54,980 --> 01:05:58,182
which happens to also be called open square bracket,

1352
01:05:58,683 --> 01:05:59,804
so I can actually run

1353
01:06:00,340 --> 01:06:00,840
which test,

1354
01:06:01,180 --> 01:06:02,821
and I can run which open square bracket.

1355
01:06:03,081 --> 01:06:04,161
These are the same program.

1356
01:06:04,721 --> 01:06:06,802
It's just a quicker way to refer to the same thing.

1357
01:06:07,182 --> 01:06:07,642
Well,

1358
01:06:07,642 --> 01:06:10,223
I can run that program as my conditional in the if,

1359
01:06:10,443 --> 01:06:14,084
and its sort of contract is that it exits with a zero if the condition is true.

1360
01:06:14,724 --> 01:06:16,425
So I can run if square bracket,

1361
01:06:16,505 --> 01:06:21,346
and then I can for example do hello equals world,

1362
01:06:22,586 --> 01:06:27,188
then echo equal else echo not equal.

1363
01:06:30,028 --> 01:06:31,129
and this will print not equal.

1364
01:06:34,209 --> 01:06:38,291
And so this way you can write actually fairly complex expressions to test,

1365
01:06:38,491 --> 01:06:40,211
and the way you understand what you do with test is well,

1366
01:06:40,212 --> 01:06:40,911
we run man test,

1367
01:06:40,912 --> 01:06:41,611
it's just a program.

1368
01:06:42,472 --> 01:06:47,733
And test explains that there's a bunch of things you could do this including things like file operations.

1369
01:06:48,313 --> 01:06:57,476
So I can do things like if dash f the date dot text then echo the date exists.

1370
01:07:00,237 --> 01:07:08,980
And the -f argument to test means that it tests whether a file by the name that you specify as the argument after

1371
01:07:09,361 --> 01:07:10,321
-f exists.

1372
01:07:11,281 --> 01:07:11,723
And so with this,

1373
01:07:11,723 --> 01:07:15,323
you can start to construct fairly complicated things,

1374
01:07:15,603 --> 01:07:17,544
automation around the programs you want to execute.

1375
01:07:17,764 --> 01:07:18,264
Yep?

1376
01:07:19,085 --> 01:07:27,088
So if this only works because there's a program that does these comparisons

1377
01:07:28,080 --> 01:07:31,082
Is if also a program?

1378
01:07:31,083 --> 01:07:31,443
Ah,

1379
01:07:31,443 --> 01:07:32,383
it's a great question.

1380
01:07:32,764 --> 01:07:33,864
Is if a program?

1381
01:07:35,506 --> 01:07:35,846
Yeah,

1382
01:07:35,846 --> 01:07:37,047
if is not a program.

1383
01:07:37,307 --> 01:07:38,348
Pipe is not a program.

1384
01:07:38,788 --> 01:07:40,849
cd is actually also not a program.

1385
01:07:41,470 --> 01:07:45,833
They are built into your shell and they are part of the bash programming language.

1386
01:07:46,634 --> 01:07:51,597
The reason why cd is not built into the shell is like a particularly interesting thing that we'll not get into,

1387
01:07:51,897 --> 01:07:54,459
but the lecture notes go into that exercise a little bit.

1388
01:07:55,500 --> 01:07:55,980
But no,

1389
01:07:55,980 --> 01:07:57,501
if is a built-in into the language,

1390
01:07:57,561 --> 01:07:58,382
same as while is.

1391
01:07:59,163 --> 01:07:59,663
Yep?

1392
01:08:00,844 --> 01:08:06,107
What does the caret C do?

1393
01:08:06,108 --> 01:08:11,931
Oh,
this is just the symbol that gets printed into my terminal when I press control C to exit the current command.

1394
01:08:12,552 --> 01:08:14,013
It doesn't do anything in and of itself.

1395
01:08:15,694 --> 01:08:17,595
It's just like a symbolism that I press that button.

1396
01:08:19,921 --> 01:08:20,922
Cool.
So that's the test program.

1397
01:08:20,962 --> 01:08:22,083
There's a bunch of things you'll often,

1398
01:08:22,283 --> 01:08:23,724
often if you read bash,

1399
01:08:23,964 --> 01:08:25,926
you'll see double square brackets instead of single ones.

1400
01:08:26,386 --> 01:08:31,110
Double square brackets are a built-in version of testing bash that is a little harder to misuse.

1401
01:08:32,171 --> 01:08:33,572
But we're not going to get into that.

1402
01:08:33,612 --> 01:08:34,693
Its syntax is very similar.

1403
01:08:36,234 --> 01:08:37,495
The last thing then is,

1404
01:08:39,837 --> 01:08:42,480
you could imagine that as you start writing these bash scripts,

1405
01:08:42,680 --> 01:08:44,141
your command line gets really long.

1406
01:08:44,701 --> 01:08:47,284
So the SSH example I gave for example,

1407
01:08:47,924 --> 01:08:51,765
is like a command that spans three lines.

1408
01:08:51,925 --> 01:08:53,826
So me going back and edit this gets pretty annoying.

1409
01:08:54,446 --> 01:08:58,607
But what you can do is you can actually take these commands and stick them into a file,

1410
01:08:59,227 --> 01:09:02,068
and that file is now a bash program that you can execute.

1411
01:09:02,588 --> 01:09:04,129
So for example in the lecture notes,

1412
01:09:04,369 --> 01:09:06,109
you'll see that there is this program right here.

1413
01:09:07,209 --> 01:09:09,850
And this is a bunch of lines of bash,

1414
01:09:10,450 --> 01:09:16,112
and when bash executes a program it will just run the lines top to bottom as if you have written them on

1415
01:09:16,512 --> 01:09:17,632
your command line at the prompt.

1416
01:09:18,533 --> 01:09:22,714
And the language is exactly the same as what you would have on your shell prompt.

1417
01:09:23,754 --> 01:09:33,737
So this particular program that is shown in the lecture notes is one that will start a stress test of your CPUs so that they get really warm and then it will run a,

1418
01:09:34,737 --> 01:09:35,198
in this case,

1419
01:09:35,198 --> 01:09:40,979
this is a Rust program that I'm running the test suite for and I want to run a particular test until that test fails.

1420
01:09:41,779 --> 01:09:45,000
And on each run I'm going to capture the log output and

1421
01:09:45,200 --> 01:09:48,344
And only when the command no longer succeeds,

1422
01:09:48,345 --> 01:09:49,265
so when the test failed,

1423
01:09:49,606 --> 01:09:53,331
only then will I kill the stress test and print out the contents of the log.

1424
01:09:53,972 --> 01:09:57,837
This is something I used in 6824 in the distributed systems class where

1425
01:09:58,238 --> 01:09:59,700
I had a bug that only

1426
01:10:00,420 --> 01:10:01,941
Test one in every 100 runs.

1427
01:10:02,561 --> 01:10:04,742
So instead of me sitting there like arrow up,

1428
01:10:04,802 --> 01:10:05,122
enter,

1429
01:10:05,122 --> 01:10:05,462
arrow up,

1430
01:10:05,462 --> 01:10:06,103
enter,
arrow up,

1431
01:10:06,103 --> 01:10:06,503
enter,

1432
01:10:06,503 --> 01:10:08,644
I would just run this once and then walk and get coffee.

1433
01:10:09,864 --> 01:10:10,765
And then when I come back,

1434
01:10:11,245 --> 01:10:13,826
there's a file on my disk that has the output of the failed test.

1435
01:10:16,587 --> 01:10:17,087
Now,

1436
01:10:17,508 --> 01:10:22,710
the bit to know about here is at the very top of this line you'll see hash and an exclamation mark.

1437
01:10:22,750 --> 01:10:26,972
This is called a shebang-- hash bang-- that is special.

1438
01:10:27,412 --> 01:10:31,173
You'll see a lot of files start with this particular instruction if they are executables.

1439
01:10:32,493 --> 01:10:37,035
This tells the shell when it runs this file,

1440
01:10:37,095 --> 01:10:38,935
if someone asks it to run this file,

1441
01:10:39,535 --> 01:10:41,836
to take this program,

1442
01:10:42,736 --> 01:10:44,537
execute the program at that path,

1443
01:10:44,997 --> 01:10:49,118
and give that program the contents of this file as input.

1444
01:10:51,299 --> 01:10:51,999
So what does that mean?

1445
01:10:52,219 --> 01:10:52,659
Well,

1446
01:10:52,659 --> 01:10:55,620
it means that if I were to run this program,

1447
01:10:59,246 --> 01:11:00,527
If I were to run this program,

1448
01:11:01,227 --> 01:11:03,970
it is equivalent-- this won't work and I'll explain why in a second.

1449
01:11:04,190 --> 01:11:05,071
I'll say permission denied.

1450
01:11:05,851 --> 01:11:07,152
But this is equivalent to

1451
01:11:08,013 --> 01:11:11,856
/bin/sh with the input being lecture.sh.

1452
01:11:13,618 --> 01:11:16,200
Those two are equivalent because that is what that shebang line means.

1453
01:11:16,941 --> 01:11:20,564
What's cool about the shebang line is that it doesn't have to be bash.

1454
01:11:20,964 --> 01:11:24,807
Bin sh here is bash but I could just as easily have said user bin python.

1455
01:11:25,688 --> 01:11:27,390
And now I can write Python code in this file.

1456
01:11:28,450 --> 01:11:30,172
And if I try to run that file,

1457
01:11:30,472 --> 01:11:31,673
it will run that Python program.

1458
01:11:31,913 --> 01:11:32,974
It will start Python,

1459
01:11:33,435 --> 01:11:37,719
take the contents of the file-- which is a valid Python program-- and give it as input to Python and then run it.

1460
01:11:38,739 --> 01:11:43,984
So you could use this for any programming language that allows textual input to be executed directly like this.

1461
01:11:44,444 --> 01:11:45,385
Also works with R,

1462
01:11:45,565 --> 01:11:46,326
also works with

1463
01:11:47,007 --> 01:11:48,248
Julia, also works with Ruby.

1464
01:11:48,908 --> 01:11:50,270
There's a bunch of languages you could do this.

1465
01:11:50,310 --> 01:11:51,971
Yeah?

1466
01:11:51,972 --> 01:11:53,092
Does it work?

1467
01:11:54,089 --> 01:11:54,589
Java.

1468
01:11:54,949 --> 01:11:56,751
Java does not allow you to do this,

1469
01:11:56,811 --> 01:11:58,472
because Java has a separate compile step.

1470
01:11:59,013 --> 01:12:01,915
So there's no one program you can run and give Java input,

1471
01:12:02,195 --> 01:12:03,877
and it will compile and run it for you.

1472
01:12:04,257 --> 01:12:04,938
But if there were,

1473
01:12:04,939 --> 01:12:06,079
and you could write such a wrapper,

1474
01:12:06,359 --> 01:12:06,720
then yes,

1475
01:12:06,720 --> 01:12:07,760
you could use it with the same thing.

1476
01:12:10,863 --> 01:12:15,066
Now,
the last bit before we terminate here is you'll see that I couldn't run the script.

1477
01:12:15,667 --> 01:12:16,687
It said permission denied.

1478
01:12:17,288 --> 01:12:20,831
This is because when you run executables,

1479
01:12:22,392 --> 01:12:24,814
the shell will check that that file,

1480
01:12:24,834 --> 01:12:26,555
that you have execute permissions on them.

1481
01:12:27,075 --> 01:12:32,838
So if I run ls -l which emits more information about every file and I give lecture.sh,

1482
01:12:33,099 --> 01:12:35,240
you'll see at the left hand side here I get some

1483
01:12:35,780 --> 01:12:36,961
Rs and W's and stuff.

1484
01:12:37,241 --> 01:12:39,342
This indicates the permissions I have on this file.

1485
01:12:39,743 --> 01:12:40,423
R means read,

1486
01:12:41,083 --> 01:12:41,744
W means write.

1487
01:12:42,388 --> 01:12:43,369
And while there are multiple,

1488
01:12:43,569 --> 01:12:44,530
there's an exercise on that.

1489
01:12:45,030 --> 01:12:47,032
But the idea here is that because this is my file,

1490
01:12:47,052 --> 01:12:47,813
I can read and write it.

1491
01:12:48,233 --> 01:12:51,716
But I haven't told the operating system that this is fine to execute,

1492
01:12:51,717 --> 01:12:53,618
that this is actually an executable program.

1493
01:12:54,158 --> 01:12:55,099
If I want to do that,

1494
01:12:55,399 --> 01:12:56,881
I have to chmod,

1495
01:12:56,921 --> 01:12:58,562
which changes the permissions of a file,

1496
01:12:59,163 --> 01:13:02,205
